```c
const struct firmware *fw;
retval = request_firmware(&fw, sst->firmware_name, sst->dev);
if (fw == NULL) {
```
Reason:
The variable `fw` is declared but not initialized before it is checked for `NULL`. If `request_firmware()` fails, `fw` remains uninitialized, leading to a potential access of an uninitialized variable when the code checks `if (fw == NULL)`, which can cause undefined behavior or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
retval = request_firmware(&fw, sst->firmware_name, sst->dev);
if (retval) {
```
Reason:
The patch changes the order of the checks by first verifying the return value of `request_firmware()`. If `retval` indicates a failure, it prevents the subsequent check of `fw`, ensuring that `fw` is only accessed when it has been properly initialized, thus avoiding the use of an uninitialized variable.

